  "divergence_notes": {
    "AZ-ID-005": "Cleave passes a tenant whose Conditional Access requires MFA everywhere; Prowler's check asks only whether security defaults are on, so it fails that tenant. Expected to disagree wherever Conditional Access is used.",
    "AZ-ID-006": "Cleave looks for a policy blocking legacy protocols; Prowler's nearest check is security defaults, which is one way of blocking them. Expected to disagree wherever Conditional Access does it instead.",
    "AZ-ID-002": "Cleave sizes the threshold to the tenant; Prowler counts Global Administrators against five.",
    "AZ-ID-003": "Cleave asks whether a privileged account is dormant; Prowler asks whether any user signed in recently.",
    "AZ-APP-001": "Cleave fails credentials valid for years; Prowler fails credentials that have already expired. The two look at the same object from opposite ends.",
    "AZ-STO-002": "Cleave's check is broader (transport, TLS and shared-key authorization); only the shared-key half has a Prowler counterpart.",
    "AZ-CMP-002": "Cleave asks about the machine (NIC or subnet); Prowler asks about the subnet alone.",
    "AZ-VULN-001": "Cleave raises a Defender vulnerability only where the machine is reachable from the internet (DECISIONS.md section 62); Prowler repeats Defender's verdict for every machine.",
    "AZ-KV-002": "Cleave fails a vault open to every network; Prowler fails a vault with no private endpoint, which is stricter.",
    "AZ-DB-001": "Cleave covers SQL and PostgreSQL public access together; the counterparts are split per engine.",
    "AWS-IAM-004": "Cleave judges the policy as a whole; Prowler has one check per clause."
  },
